-- title:
--   Riemann–Roch for the modular function field over ℚ̄
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number, let $K = \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ be the algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$, and let $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldBar N` be the intermediate field of $K((q))$ over $K$ obtained by base change: it is the $K$-subfield of $K((q))$ generated by the image, under the coefficientwise embedding of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ into $K((q))$, of `modularFunctionFieldFull N`, itself the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the expansions `divisorExpansions N`. The assertion is `FunctionFieldRiemannRoch K F`, that is: assuming $F$ is a curve over $K$ (principal divisors exist, every place has residue field finite over $K$, and $\Omega[F/K]$ is free of rank one over $F$), that canonical divisors exist (every nonzero $\omega \in \Omega[F/K]$ has a divisor whose value at each place $v$ is $\mathrm{ord}_v$ of the differential $\omega$), and that at every place the element $v.\mathrm{dCoord}$ spans $\Omega[F/K]$ over $F$, then for every nonzero $\omega \in \Omega[F/K]$ and every divisor $D$ (a finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued function on the places of $F$ over $K$, places being proper valuation subrings of $F$ containing $K$ that are principal ideal rings),
--   $$\ell(D) - \ell\big((\omega) - D\big) = \deg D + 1 - g,$$
--   where $(\omega)$ is the canonical divisor attached to $\omega$, $\deg$ weights each place by its degree, and $g$ is the genus of $F/K$, defined as $(\deg(\omega') + 2)/2$ for a chosen nonzero differential $\omega'$.
--
--   This is the Riemann–Roch theorem for the function field of the modular curve $X_0(N)$ with algebraically closed constant field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, obtained by specialising the general Riemann–Roch theorem over an algebraically closed base field to this field, whose relevant structural hypotheses (curve over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, essential finite type, generation by $j$ and $j_N$ with $j$ transcendental and $j_N$ integral over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}(j)$) are supplied by the cited results. It underlies the subsequent computations of genus, of degrees of canonical divisors and of dimensions of Riemann–Roch spaces on $X_0(N)$, and of height estimates made with functions in such spaces.
